Wait — about "the isTyping" guard: mousedown has no isTyping check (mousedown doesn't need it. Typing happens in inputs, and mousedown on a canvas while typing is impossible. mousedown targets the canvas element). The mousedown handler only has the `anySurfaceOpen()` and `selfShipRef.current` guards. For touch buttons, since a user can't be typing into an input while pressing a touch button, the typing guard isn't strictly necessary for touch. But the spec says "extract the body of the canvas mousedown fire handler (roughly lines 1457–1486: isTyping/anySurfaceOpen/selfShipRef guards...)". The mousedown handler doesn't actually have an isTyping guard — the spec is loose about it. I'll preserve the existing guards: anySurfaceOpen + selfShipRef.

Note the spec: "canvas mousedown handler becomes `onDown = (e) => { if (e.button === 0) fireWeapon(); }` (preserving existing guards)".

About the '1'/'2' key weapon selection — the spec says the touch weapon buttons should call "the same code path as the '1'/'2' keys (setWeapon + weaponRef)". So I'll extract a `selectWeapon(w: WeaponId)` function:

```tsx
const selectWeapon = (w: WeaponId): void => {
  setWeapon(w);
  weaponRef.current = w;
};
```

The key handler will call `selectWeapon('laser')` / `selectWeapon('missile')`, and the CombatHud's `onWeapon` prop will call `selectWeapon(w)`. And the TouchControls weapon buttons will call selectWeapon.

About the 'T' key target lock — extract `toggleTargetLockAction()`:

```tsx
const toggleTargetLockAction = (): void => {
  if (anySurfaceOpen()) return;
  if (!selfShipRef.current) return;
  const cmd = toggleTargetLock(Date.now());
  if (!cmd) return;
  if (cmd.type === 'lock') {
    clientRef.current?.send('target_lock', { targetId: cmd.targetId });
  } else {
    clientRef.current?.send('target_release', {});
  }
};
```

The key handler becomes: `if (e.key !== 't'...) return; if (e.repeat) return; [input guard]; toggleTargetLockAction();`

